{
  "configuration_id": "config_009",
  "reason": "Evidence: The best config so far (config_001: scgpt + imagenet_vit_l + avgpool + weighted, multiprofile) achieves 0.542 accuracy, but the RNA-only baseline (config_036: scgpt + avgpool) reaches 0.530, meaning the image modality contributes only ~1.2%. All multimodal tests have used imagenet_vit_l, leaving the image encoder choice unexplored. Weighted fusion clearly outperforms concat (0.542 vs 0.518), and avgpool beats transformer aggregation (0.542 vs 0.520) with this RNA encoder. Question: Will switching the image encoder to ResNet50 (which captures different morphological/spatial features than a ViT) improve the multimodal contribution beyond the current 1.2% gain, while keeping all other settings at their best-performing values? Why this is the best next experiment: It is a clean single-variable change from the current best, directly testing whether the modest image benefit is encoder-specific. ResNet50 is substantially smaller than ViT-L (~23M vs ~300M params), so the expected training cost is lower—roughly 1.5–2.5 GPU hours vs. the 2.94 for config_001—making it a cost-efficient exploration. If it matches or beats 0.542, we gain a cheaper best config; if it underperforms, we confirm ViT-L is the right image encoder and can focus remaining budget on other dimensions (RNA encoder, profile setting)."
}

